  "operation": {
    "kind": "prompt",
    "user": "Write `verified_by` onto the docs each acceptance test defends, and report what is left\nunverified.\n\n## The tests\n\n{{.inputs.acceptance_tests}}\n\n## What the run reported\n\n{{.inputs.results}}\n\n## The deliverables they were supposed to defend\n\n{{.inputs.features}}\n\n## What to do\n\n**For every deliverable with a PASSING test**, add a `verified_by` entry to its product\ndoc naming the test and where it lives. This is the durable half of the traceability\nspine: a reader arriving next quarter, with no run in front of them, can follow the\ndeliverable to the assertion that defends it.\n\nDo the same on any component doc a test walks through, so a component's page says which\nend-to-end test would notice if it broke.\n\n**A failing test does not earn a `verified_by` line.** Nor does a test that exists but\nwas skipped. Only a passing assertion verifies anything.\n\n## What to report\n\n`unverified` — one row per claim with no passing test: the deliverable id, its success\nsignal, and which of these it is:\n\n- **no test was written** for it\n- a test exists and **failed**\n- a test exists and was **skipped**\n- it **cannot be driven** automatically, and a manual procedure covers it\n\nThat last one is not a gap; the others are. Every row here reaches the gate as something\na person is asked about regardless of the confidence score, so be accurate rather than\ngenerous — a claim marked verified on a skipped test is the one failure mode this whole\nphase exists to prevent.\n\n## What to produce\n\n`verified_by` — one row per doc edited: the doc, the deliverable, the test, its path.\n\n`unverified` — as above.\n\n`paths` — every doc you edited.\n",
    "config": {
      "model": "claude-cli/default"
    },
